Q: How to detect a Windows network path? A: <p>I agree that the simplest check is <code>yourpathstring.startswith(r'\\')</code>.</p>
<p>However, this will be a false-positive for any linux path which starts with <code>\\</code> (unlikely but possible) and a false-negative when people use forward slashes: <code>//server\share\folder</code>.</p>
<p>It appears that the library function <code>splitdrive</code> was designed with network path support in mind. The network part is considered &quot;drive&quot;, so just have to check if it's a non-DOS drive:</p>
<pre><code>import os

def is_network_path(path):
    drive = os.path.splitdrive(path)[0]
    if drive:
        return not drive.endswith(':')
    else:
        return False
</code></pre>
<p>Probably not worth the effort, but it &quot;feels&quot; more correct.</p>

Q: Why do we need interfaces in Java? A: <p>Interfaces are essential for loose coupling. In real world applications, there are certain features that can be implemented in different ways. These features are used by the application and their implementation can have changes frequently. Interfaces provide a way for the applications to use the FEATURE without caring about its implementation.</p>
<p>Let me elaborate with an example: Suppose an application requires a payment. This requires a payment method. Now this can be any payment method like credit card, debit card, in-app coins. There can be additional methods added later. How will you feed the payment method without the app worrying about the implementation. Here we use interface. We create a payment interface with neccessary behaviours to be implemented. So instead of individually providing: paypal_payment(), debitcard_payment(), etc... we provide payment() method referencing the Payment interface. This lets the app use payment() method without worrying about the exact implementation. As long as payment method is implemented in some way, this will work.</p>
<pre class="lang-java prettyprint-override"><code>interface Payment{

    void payment(){}

}

class Paypal implements Payment{

    void payment(){// One implementation of payment using paypal}

}

class DebitCard implements Payment{

    void payment(){ // Code for payment using debit card}  

}

class App{

void payment_process(Payment p1){

    p1.payment(); // It doesn't matter if payment method of Debitcard or paypal is implemented
}
</code></pre>
<p>Note that I provided object of <code>Payment</code> interface inside the App class, not the object of <code>DebitCard</code> or <code>Paypal</code>. This helps to achieve polymorphism according to the required payment method.</p>
<p>So in real world, what any application focus on is the FEATURE itself. What is the feature? Interface tells exactly that without focusing on how it is achieved. Another quick example can be: For TV we need to change volume, it doesn't matter if we use remote or TV button or mobile app. So the feature of <code>changeVolume()</code> can be an interface and any way of implementing that interface is valid. We can pass this interface to our app instead of passing <code>buttontoChangeVolume()</code>, <code>remoteToChangeVolume()</code>, <code>bluetoothToChangeVolume()</code> separately.</p>

Q: How to access bash variable in python script via hql file(bash-hql-python) A: <pre><code>@echo off
setlocal EnableExtensions

REM Python runtime
set &quot;PYTHON_EXE=C:\Programs\Miniconda-25.5.1-py3135\python.exe&quot;

REM Validate Python runtime
if not exist &quot;%PYTHON_EXE%&quot; (
    echo [%DATE% %TIME%] ERROR: Python executable not found: &quot;%PYTHON_EXE%&quot;
    exit /b 10
)

REM Application script is one directory above this scripts directory
set &quot;APP_SCRIPT=%~dp0..\ndm_file_transfer.py&quot;

REM Validate application script
if not exist &quot;%APP_SCRIPT%&quot; (
    echo [%DATE% %TIME%] ERROR: Application script not found: &quot;%APP_SCRIPT%&quot;
    exit /b 11
)

echo [%DATE% %TIME%] INFO: Starting NDM
echo [%DATE% %TIME%] INFO: Python: &quot;%PYTHON_EXE%&quot;

&quot;%PYTHON_EXE%&quot; &quot;%APP_SCRIPT%&quot; %*

set &quot;RC=%ERRORLEVEL%&quot;

echo [%DATE% %TIME%] INFO: NDM completed with return code %RC%

exit /b %RC%
</code></pre>
